1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Digging, dragging, scraping, hauling, and dumping.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:11,000
It's a drama of monumental logistics,

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:16,000
often involving some of the most powerful mechanized monsters ever devised.

4
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:21,000
Now, Earthmovers on Modern Marvelous.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
From simple hand tools to gargantuan machines,

6
00:00:37,001 --> 00:00:40,000
along with high-powered explosives,

7
00:00:40,001 --> 00:00:43,000
we have literally moved mountains

8
00:00:43,001 --> 00:00:47,000
and sifted through the earth beneath our feet.

9
00:00:47,001 --> 00:00:51,000
In the last hundred years, we humans using new inventions

10
00:00:51,001 --> 00:00:53,000
have moved more cubic yards of earth

11
00:00:53,001 --> 00:00:57,000
than our predecessors did in the previous 100,000 years.

12
00:00:57,001 --> 00:01:02,000
in the previous 100,000 years.

13
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:05,000
At any given moment, there are major earth-moving projects

14
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:07,940
in progress all over the globe.

15
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:10,940
On the south side of Las Vegas, the fastest growing city

16
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:12,940
in America, the hills are being readied

17
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:14,860
for a new residential neighborhood.

18
00:01:18,960 --> 00:01:21,960
This particular project, which is one of the major projects

19
00:01:22,060 --> 00:01:24,960
we have going in the Las Vegas Valley right now,

20
00:01:24,961 --> 00:01:28,960
currently has just under 100 pieces of equipment

21
00:01:28,961 --> 00:01:31,960
that varies from very large bulldozers to small bulldozers

22
00:01:31,961 --> 00:01:33,960
to motor graders to scrapers

23
00:01:33,961 --> 00:01:36,960
and quite a few water pools for dust control.

24
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:42,960
All the material up here on the hill stays in the construction region.

25
00:01:42,961 --> 00:01:47,960
Some areas have large cuts, other areas have large fills.

26
00:01:47,961 --> 00:01:50,960
You're cutting soil from one area and banking it in another area

27
00:01:50,961 --> 00:01:53,960
in a compacted state so they can build houses up there.

28
00:01:54,960 --> 00:01:58,560
The American Asphalt and Grading Company uses trucks,

29
00:01:58,561 --> 00:02:01,880
some capable of hauling up to 100 tons in a single load,

30
00:02:02,140 --> 00:02:04,540
to transport the earth from where it's being cut

31
00:02:04,541 --> 00:02:06,140
to where it's needed to fill.

32
00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:09,720
One of the most challenging things we're running into

33
00:02:09,721 --> 00:02:11,160
is the type of soil.

34
00:02:11,260 --> 00:02:13,360
As you can see behind us, it is very rocky

35
00:02:13,361 --> 00:02:15,180
and very difficult to dig.

36
00:02:16,760 --> 00:02:20,560
That's why two Komatsu D575A-2s,

37
00:02:21,660 --> 00:02:24,040
known as super rivers, were brought to this site.

38
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:27,960
They're the largest and most productive ripping and dozing

39
00:02:27,961 --> 00:02:29,960
machines in the world.

40
00:02:31,960 --> 00:02:36,960
These machines have an operating weight of around 336,000 pounds,

41
00:02:36,961 --> 00:02:39,960
are over 40 feet long and 16 feet tall.

42
00:02:39,961 --> 00:02:43,960
The basic ripping function is to be able to put your ripper point

43
00:02:43,961 --> 00:02:44,960
below the material.

44
00:02:44,961 --> 00:02:48,960
You need to penetrate that material by lifting the rear of the tractor.

45
00:02:48,961 --> 00:02:52,960
Once you are into the material, we pull that ripper forward using

46
00:02:52,961 --> 00:02:57,160
forward using a Komatsu 12V 1,050 horsepower engine.

47
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:03,300
Super rippers can be equipped with up to three ripper blades

48
00:03:03,301 --> 00:03:05,600
called shanks.

49
00:03:05,601 --> 00:03:08,600
In the very rocky terrain of the Las Vegas housing project,

50
00:03:08,601 --> 00:03:11,720
there's too much resistance to rip with three shanks.

51
00:03:11,721 --> 00:03:14,020
So only one is being used in this application.

52
00:03:17,820 --> 00:03:20,420
When these machines are done ripping,

53
00:03:20,421 --> 00:03:25,360
They will move the material using the 78 cubic yard blade into the correct location.

54
00:03:28,780 --> 00:03:32,340
One of the primary differences in earth moving equipment today and

55
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:38,380
25 years ago when I entered in the industry is the complexity of the equipment and the productivity of the equipment.

56
00:03:38,940 --> 00:03:44,800
The older equipment was a lot simpler, but not nearly as productive as the equipment we have today.

57
00:03:45,060 --> 00:03:49,060
The equipment itself is much more user-friendly for the operator.

58
00:03:49,061 --> 00:03:50,720
The creature comforts are incredible now

59
00:03:50,820 --> 00:03:54,360
compared to what they used to be.

60
00:03:54,460 --> 00:03:56,460
At the site of another future housing development

61
00:03:56,560 --> 00:03:59,980
in Southern California, the Sukit Construction Company

62
00:04:00,080 --> 00:04:02,800
also has to contend with a rocky environment,

63
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:05,240
presenting the same challenges as those encountered in Las

64
00:04:05,340 --> 00:04:07,940
Vegas.

65
00:04:08,040 --> 00:04:09,480
One of the special pieces of equipment

66
00:04:09,580 --> 00:04:11,820
they've chosen to help accomplish the task

67
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:14,280
is the Caterpillar 5130 excavator.

68
00:04:19,060 --> 00:04:22,060
We love this tractor just for its efficiency.

69
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:24,700
It doesn't require a lot of support

70
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:26,060
when you're running this tractor.

71
00:04:26,160 --> 00:04:27,000
It's self-contained.

72
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:31,200
These are your control pedals.

73
00:04:31,300 --> 00:04:33,500
You can either move it forward and backwards by feet,

74
00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:35,440
or you can do it by hand, whatever one is more

75
00:04:35,540 --> 00:04:36,920
comfortable for you at the time.

76
00:04:37,020 --> 00:04:38,820
This is your hoist for the bucket.

77
00:04:38,920 --> 00:04:42,280
It makes the boom go up and down, and then it also

78
00:04:42,380 --> 00:04:44,660
curls the bucket in and out.

79
00:04:44,760 --> 00:04:49,060
The 5130 excavator weighs in at a hefty 400 feet.

80
00:04:49,061 --> 00:04:53,660
and can load a truck capable of holding 100 tons of material

81
00:04:53,661 --> 00:04:55,460
with just a few shovel loads.

82
00:05:06,720 --> 00:05:09,920
It has a 13.6 cubic yard bucket,

83
00:05:09,921 --> 00:05:15,060
and it'll lift the 30 to 35,000 pounds.

84
00:05:15,061 --> 00:05:23,240
We can dig approximately 6,000 to 8,000 cubic yards of dirt a day with this tractor.

85
00:05:25,280 --> 00:05:31,500
This excavator has been outfitted with two antennae to pick up signals from global positioning system satellites.

86
00:05:35,540 --> 00:05:42,100
Using GPS-based computer technology, the operator can determine exactly where and how much to dig.

87
00:05:45,060 --> 00:05:49,100
The operator, all he has to do is look at the computer screen

88
00:05:49,101 --> 00:05:52,020
to know where he is, grade off the light bars,

89
00:05:52,660 --> 00:05:57,100
and he doesn't need a person telling him where to grade.

90
00:05:57,620 --> 00:06:00,520
And from a safety standpoint, that's pretty beneficial as well.

91
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:05,060
Overall, this project demands the movement

92
00:06:05,061 --> 00:06:07,540
of over 4 million cubic yards of earth.

93
00:06:08,540 --> 00:06:11,280
If you were to take the operation that you see behind me running,

94
00:06:11,380 --> 00:06:13,140
which is about 15,000 yards a day,

95
00:06:13,141 --> 00:06:16,140
If it assumed that a man and a wheelbarrow were used,

96
00:06:16,240 --> 00:06:19,480
it would take between 10,000 and 20,000 men with wheelbarrows

97
00:06:19,580 --> 00:06:22,380
to haul the same amount of material that we're hauling.

98
00:06:22,480 --> 00:06:25,180
Modern machines have made it possible to easily tackle

99
00:06:25,280 --> 00:06:26,880
mammoth earth-moving projects.

100
00:06:29,720 --> 00:06:32,500
Case in point, in Southern California,

101
00:06:32,600 --> 00:06:35,660
the three recently completed dams holding back Diamond Valley

102
00:06:35,760 --> 00:06:39,760
Lake's 260 billion gallons of water.

103
00:06:39,860 --> 00:06:41,800
They represent the largest earthworks project

104
00:06:41,801 --> 00:06:45,800
in the history of the United States.

105
00:06:45,900 --> 00:06:48,840
Excavation began back in 1995.

106
00:06:48,940 --> 00:06:51,440
The entire reservoir project,

107
00:06:51,540 --> 00:06:55,140
including the East Dam and the West Dam, which we have,

108
00:06:55,240 --> 00:06:59,060
is approximately 150 million cubic yards.

109
00:06:59,160 --> 00:07:01,260
Planters had decided that a new reservoir

110
00:07:01,360 --> 00:07:04,060
was vital for Southern California's future,

111
00:07:04,160 --> 00:07:07,360
since the existing pipeline that comes from the Colorado River,

112
00:07:07,460 --> 00:07:10,100
248 miles to the east,

113
00:07:10,101 --> 00:07:13,260
crosses the earthquake rift zone of the San Andreas Fault.

114
00:07:15,560 --> 00:07:18,700
Water engineers figured that one day the big one would strike

115
00:07:18,701 --> 00:07:21,560
and this vital supply line might be cut off.

116
00:07:22,820 --> 00:07:25,660
They resolved that this heavily populated and arid region

117
00:07:25,661 --> 00:07:27,620
required a reserve supply of water.

118
00:07:31,100 --> 00:07:32,800
Completed in only four years,

119
00:07:33,300 --> 00:07:35,520
the speed with which this new reservoir was built

120
00:07:35,521 --> 00:07:38,420
wouldn't have been possible without today's state-of-the-art equipment.

121
00:07:38,421 --> 00:07:43,420
This includes the water trucks used for dust control and soil compaction.

122
00:07:43,421 --> 00:07:48,420
We haul 20,000 gallons per load.

123
00:07:48,421 --> 00:07:53,420
And what really amazes you is the way that the computer programs and so much of the systems

124
00:07:53,421 --> 00:08:02,420
are controlled by electronic modules and computerization where we can download information to tell basically

125
00:08:02,421 --> 00:08:07,420
what that machine, the function of that machine for an entire 24-hour period.

126
00:08:07,421 --> 00:08:09,080
We never had that before.

127
00:08:09,620 --> 00:08:11,760
We thought we knew it all, but we didn't.

128
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:14,600
To help break up solid rock,

129
00:08:14,960 --> 00:08:19,120
huge Ingersoll Rand drills created holes 45 feet deep,

130
00:08:19,340 --> 00:08:21,260
which were then packed with explosives.

131
00:08:25,360 --> 00:08:27,300
After the charges were detonated,

132
00:08:28,040 --> 00:08:31,460
a caterpillar shovel weighing 350 tons

133
00:08:31,461 --> 00:08:34,440
moved in and scooped up the earth and rock

134
00:08:34,441 --> 00:08:36,720
into enormous trucks that hauled it away.

135
00:08:37,420 --> 00:08:41,100
So every pass, he's putting 23 cubic yards into that truck.

136
00:08:42,120 --> 00:08:45,000
He can load the truck in a minute and 15 seconds,

137
00:08:45,240 --> 00:08:46,360
four to five passes,

138
00:08:46,520 --> 00:08:48,560
depending upon the density of the material that he's loading.

139
00:08:49,340 --> 00:08:53,000
Every shovel full of earth weighed over 90,000 pounds.

140
00:08:55,140 --> 00:08:58,300
When you look at all the large-scale earth-moving projects today

141
00:08:58,301 --> 00:09:02,380
and the amount of equipment it takes to get the jobs done,

142
00:09:02,620 --> 00:09:04,680
it just makes it all the more amazing.

143
00:09:04,681 --> 00:09:07,880
The massive building projects in ancient times,

144
00:09:07,980 --> 00:09:14,060
to do this without machines, it's just mind boggling.

145
00:09:14,160 --> 00:09:17,660
Eons ago, Earthmoving's earliest innovators

146
00:09:17,760 --> 00:09:23,940
fashioned their first tools, a shovel, a pick, and a hoe.

147
00:09:24,040 --> 00:09:26,240
The next step forward in the development of Earthmoving

148
00:09:26,340 --> 00:09:29,480
equipment occurred when some unknown dreamer got the idea

149
00:09:29,580 --> 00:09:32,980
of putting boards together between two shafts

150
00:09:32,981 --> 00:09:35,980
to form the first handbarrow.

151
00:09:35,981 --> 00:09:38,980
Not content with this arrangement, another ancient inventor,

152
00:09:38,981 --> 00:09:42,980
probably in China, got the idea of attaching a wheel to one end,

153
00:09:42,981 --> 00:09:45,980
and the first wheelbarrow was born.

154
00:09:45,981 --> 00:09:49,980
In ancient Egypt, over 4,000 years ago,

155
00:09:49,981 --> 00:09:54,980
something resembling a sled was used to haul heavy burdens.

156
00:09:54,981 --> 00:09:57,980
Round sticks or logs were placed underneath it

157
00:09:57,981 --> 00:09:59,980
to diminish friction.

158
00:09:59,981 --> 00:10:04,980
Now, more dirt or big rocks could be hauled.

159
00:10:04,981 --> 00:10:11,980
Between 250 and 200 BC, the Romans used similar methods on their vast highway system.

160
00:10:11,981 --> 00:10:16,980
But the development of earth-moving tools was extremely slow.

161
00:10:16,981 --> 00:10:22,980
The same basic practical two-wheeled cart had been used by the Egyptians

162
00:10:22,981 --> 00:10:28,980
and was later adopted by Europe's medieval peasants and then early American farmers.

163
00:10:28,981 --> 00:10:35,760
The only real innovation was replacing or augmenting manpower with horse, ox, or mule power.

164
00:10:37,140 --> 00:10:42,380
Around the time of the American Revolution, earth-moving tools began to change.

165
00:10:43,660 --> 00:10:51,500
The Ames family of Massachusetts transformed one of the earliest and basically unchanged earth-moving tools, the shovel.

166
00:10:54,200 --> 00:10:58,200
Their modified shovel was four pounds lighter than its old British counterpart.

167
00:10:58,980 --> 00:11:01,480
Although it looked like its handmade ancestor,

168
00:11:01,481 --> 00:11:05,220
this shovel was factory made, featuring steel plate

169
00:11:05,221 --> 00:11:08,480
with welded iron straps.

170
00:11:08,481 --> 00:11:11,820
By the turn of the 19th century, the Ames family

171
00:11:11,821 --> 00:11:15,320
was turning out 20,000 shovels a year.

172
00:11:15,321 --> 00:11:18,640
As the century progressed, America charged forward

173
00:11:18,641 --> 00:11:21,140
into a new age of industrialization

174
00:11:21,141 --> 00:11:23,640
that would include earth-moving machines

175
00:11:23,641 --> 00:11:27,480
that no one could have imagined.

176
00:11:27,481 --> 00:11:30,480
The Kimberly Diamond Mine in South Africa

177
00:11:30,481 --> 00:11:33,480
was the largest hand-dug excavation in the world.

178
00:11:33,481 --> 00:11:36,480
By the time work ended in 1914,

179
00:11:36,481 --> 00:11:41,480
22.5 million tons of earth had been excavated.

180
00:11:41,481 --> 00:11:45,480
Earthmovers will return on Modern Marvels.

181
00:11:49,480 --> 00:11:52,480
Today's earth-moving giants are sometimes built

182
00:11:52,481 --> 00:11:55,480
on a seemingly impossible scale.

183
00:11:57,480 --> 00:12:00,820
When seen digging for coal in this European mine,

184
00:12:01,440 --> 00:12:04,140
operated by the RWE Power Company of Germany,

185
00:12:05,160 --> 00:12:08,100
these bucket-wheel excavators are obviously impressive.

186
00:12:08,720 --> 00:12:11,260
But when they make an appearance in the everyday world,

187
00:12:11,720 --> 00:12:13,780
their size can seem even more overwhelming,

188
00:12:14,440 --> 00:12:18,120
like the time this monster crossed a road on its way to a new dig site.

189
00:12:19,120 --> 00:12:23,480
One of the most famous behemoths was nicknamed Big Muskie.

190
00:12:23,481 --> 00:12:28,780
It was manufactured in 1969 for use in an Ohio coal mine

191
00:12:28,781 --> 00:12:32,280
and was nearly 22 stories high.

192
00:12:34,040 --> 00:12:39,000
This Colossus weighed more than 150 Boeing 727 jet planes

193
00:12:39,001 --> 00:12:42,440
and was the largest dragline excavator in the world.

194
00:12:44,980 --> 00:12:49,180
A dragline offers the advantage of a much longer reach than a power shovel.

195
00:12:49,181 --> 00:12:59,160
Using its long boom and cables, it works from the top of an excavation by dropping its bucket down and then filling it by dragging it back up the sides.

196
00:13:01,980 --> 00:13:06,220
Big Muskie could scoop up 325 tons of earth at a time.

197
00:13:07,420 --> 00:13:13,640
And then, with a swing of its 310-foot-long boom, dump its load 600 feet away.

198
00:13:14,980 --> 00:13:17,360
Design and build by Bucira Siri.

199
00:13:17,361 --> 00:13:25,000
Big Muskie swung a bucket that was 220 cubic yards in capacity.

200
00:13:25,560 --> 00:13:29,520
Now, it's hard to imagine what is 220 cubic yards.

201
00:13:30,200 --> 00:13:36,100
That bucket was large enough to park two Greyhound buses side by side in it.

202
00:13:38,520 --> 00:13:42,820
Big Muskie used more electrical power than a city of 100,000 people.

203
00:13:42,821 --> 00:13:48,800
In 1991, after it had served for 23 years in the coal mine,

204
00:13:49,760 --> 00:13:52,860
environmental concerns about the mine's high sulfur content

205
00:13:52,861 --> 00:13:54,840
compelled its owners to shut it down.

206
00:13:57,360 --> 00:13:59,220
In the years that it was operational,

207
00:13:59,580 --> 00:14:02,880
Big Muskie removed nearly 500 million cubic yards of earth

208
00:14:02,881 --> 00:14:06,080
and uncovered 18 million tons of coal.

209
00:14:07,780 --> 00:14:11,820
The basic principle behind the drag line dates all the way back to 1836.

210
00:14:12,820 --> 00:14:18,580
when the 363-mile-long Erie Canal was under construction across upstate New York.

211
00:14:20,960 --> 00:14:25,680
A new type of digging tool was invented by having draft animals pull a strip of iron

212
00:14:25,681 --> 00:14:29,600
that acted as a cutting edge to scrape up loose dirt and rocks.

213
00:14:30,340 --> 00:14:32,160
It was called a drag scraper.

214
00:14:34,200 --> 00:14:38,160
Around this time, one of the transportation dreamers who spearheaded the canal,

215
00:14:38,760 --> 00:14:43,020
Robert Fulton, also advanced efforts to harness the power of steam.

216
00:14:44,200 --> 00:14:47,320
His Claremont steamboat was the first to steam up the Hudson.

217
00:14:48,260 --> 00:14:50,980
Soon after, steam power began moving Earth as well.

218
00:14:52,160 --> 00:14:53,520
In the history of Earth moving,

219
00:14:54,060 --> 00:14:59,780
there's a distinct line between manpower and animal power and mechanization.

220
00:15:01,160 --> 00:15:04,920
In 1835, a gentleman by the name of William Otis

221
00:15:04,921 --> 00:15:07,440
built the first steam shovel.

222
00:15:07,441 --> 00:15:11,680
that would usher in a whole era of new machines.

223
00:15:13,140 --> 00:15:17,760
William Otis developed this new machine called the Yankee Geologist,

224
00:15:17,960 --> 00:15:21,040
was the name originally dubbed onto it,

225
00:15:21,260 --> 00:15:24,460
and it's quite surprising to see pictures of that old machine

226
00:15:24,461 --> 00:15:29,360
and look at the modern machines and see how similar they are in overall appearance.

227
00:15:31,460 --> 00:15:34,360
Around the time William Otis' steam shovel design

228
00:15:34,361 --> 00:15:37,160
was finally put into mass production in the 1880s,

229
00:15:37,440 --> 00:15:40,500
Other early ancestors of well-known earth-moving machines

230
00:15:40,580 --> 00:15:43,680
were being created.

231
00:15:43,740 --> 00:15:47,120
The same steam shovel technology was used in dredges,

232
00:15:47,180 --> 00:15:49,920
machines designed to scoop earth from the rivers and harbors

233
00:15:49,980 --> 00:15:54,120
of a growing industrial America.

234
00:15:54,200 --> 00:15:56,420
It was the Foley brothers that hit upon the idea

235
00:15:56,500 --> 00:15:59,360
of using a team of animals to push a steel plate

236
00:15:59,420 --> 00:16:02,500
instead of drawing it from behind.

237
00:16:02,560 --> 00:16:04,560
This was the early prototype of what

238
00:16:04,561 --> 00:16:07,560
of what was later to become the bulldozer.

239
00:16:10,560 --> 00:16:13,560
By the 1890s, the Austin Company of Chicago

240
00:16:13,561 --> 00:16:16,560
was perfecting a dump wagon with a hinged bottom,

241
00:16:16,561 --> 00:16:19,560
which could be used to quickly pour out an entire load.

242
00:16:19,561 --> 00:16:24,560
Soon, companies like Mack Trucks, founded in New York in 1902,

243
00:16:24,561 --> 00:16:27,560
and others were quick to seize upon the possibility

244
00:16:27,561 --> 00:16:30,560
of combining the internal combustion gasoline engine

245
00:16:30,561 --> 00:16:32,560
with the wagon design.

246
00:16:32,561 --> 00:16:37,220
these new vehicles would lead the way to today's mega-sized dump trucks.

247
00:16:38,840 --> 00:16:40,920
At the dawn of the 20th century,

248
00:16:41,340 --> 00:16:45,320
President Theodore Roosevelt spearheaded the construction of a canal across Panama,

249
00:16:46,120 --> 00:16:48,100
connecting the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans.

250
00:16:49,940 --> 00:16:51,600
As an earth-moving challenge,

251
00:16:51,601 --> 00:16:57,180
the canal was made possible by the combination of the steam shovel and the railroad car.

252
00:16:59,100 --> 00:17:02,540
During the process of that job,

253
00:17:02,560 --> 00:17:16,560
Between 1904 and 1914, it required 102 steam shovels to dig approximately 255 million cubic yards of earth,

254
00:17:16,940 --> 00:17:20,180
which is just astounding for that time period.

255
00:17:21,700 --> 00:17:27,680
These steam shovels were made primarily by two manufacturers, Bucyrus and Marion.

256
00:17:27,681 --> 00:17:30,860
The transportation of the material was rail transport.

257
00:17:30,861 --> 00:17:37,540
they laid temporary rail tracks and rail cars were used with steam locomotives to haul the material away from the major cuttings

258
00:17:38,400 --> 00:17:46,020
And as we all know the canal opened on time in 1914 and has been a major seaway ever since

259
00:17:48,720 --> 00:17:53,560
At the time the canal was being dug a major breakthrough occurred in the farmlands of California

260
00:17:53,561 --> 00:17:56,560
California.

261
00:17:56,561 --> 00:17:59,560
Agricultural equipment manufacturer Benjamin Holt

262
00:17:59,561 --> 00:18:05,320
came west in 1883 and set up shop in Stockton, California.

263
00:18:05,321 --> 00:18:08,320
His lifelong rival, Daniel Best, was a gold miner,

264
00:18:08,321 --> 00:18:11,160
Hunter, and Miller, who tramped all over the west

265
00:18:11,161 --> 00:18:13,060
before settling in San Leandro.

266
00:18:15,820 --> 00:18:19,520
Both men realized that unlike the flat plains of the Midwest,

267
00:18:19,521 --> 00:18:23,440
California's topography called for an improved combine that

268
00:18:23,441 --> 00:18:26,040
to bring more marginal land under cultivation.

269
00:18:28,380 --> 00:18:31,400
Holt came up with a new kind of side hill harvester.

270
00:18:32,300 --> 00:18:33,960
Best picked up the challenge

271
00:18:33,961 --> 00:18:36,260
and soon the two were locked in a contest

272
00:18:36,261 --> 00:18:39,340
to see who could turn out the most gargantuan machine.

273
00:18:41,260 --> 00:18:43,120
As their rival machines went into action,

274
00:18:43,121 --> 00:18:46,220
they boosted wheat production by millions of bushels.

275
00:18:47,760 --> 00:18:50,720
The early tractors were just huge.

276
00:18:50,721 --> 00:18:54,540
You'll see pictures of ones with huge A-frames built and extended wheels,

277
00:18:55,460 --> 00:18:58,680
which of course meant that when you got those stuck too,

278
00:18:58,740 --> 00:19:00,260
it was just a bigger job to get them out.

279
00:19:01,700 --> 00:19:06,380
Bigger wheels were no solution in the spongy soil around Stockton in the rainy season.

280
00:19:07,500 --> 00:19:11,520
Hold is credited with coming up with the idea that if you could have,

281
00:19:11,700 --> 00:19:14,980
like a set of planks that just laid themselves down in front of you,

282
00:19:15,080 --> 00:19:17,220
spread your weight out so you could stay on top of the bog,

283
00:19:17,600 --> 00:19:19,660
developing the first track layer system.

284
00:19:20,720 --> 00:19:27,780
Holt and his men removed the wheels from steam traction engine number 77 and installed a pair of new track units.

285
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,880
The date was November 24, 1904.

286
00:19:32,840 --> 00:19:37,920
A temporary solution to a local problem would soon be multiplied millions of times.

287
00:19:38,820 --> 00:19:42,560
When a photographer said the movements of the new machines reminded him of a Caterpillar,

288
00:19:43,300 --> 00:19:46,480
the name stuck and the Caterpillar brand was born.

289
00:19:46,481 --> 00:19:53,920
Holt and Best, always rivals, now intensified their competition, each company perfecting

290
00:19:53,921 --> 00:19:58,340
its design to outsell the other.

291
00:19:58,341 --> 00:20:04,080
These two talented inventor entrepreneurs not only mastered the art of production, but

292
00:20:04,081 --> 00:20:09,240
also used new techniques of salesmanship to sell their machines throughout the world,

293
00:20:09,241 --> 00:20:16,280
launching a new era in earthmoving.

294
00:20:16,281 --> 00:20:19,780
When Big Muskie was dismantled in 1999,

295
00:20:19,781 --> 00:20:22,380
enough steel was salvaged to have manufactured

296
00:20:22,381 --> 00:20:26,180
more than 9,000 automobiles.

297
00:20:26,181 --> 00:20:29,440
Earthmovers will return on Modern Marvels,

298
00:20:29,441 --> 00:20:31,080
here on the History Channel.

299
00:20:35,880 --> 00:20:39,880
The earthmoving industry originally relied on muscle

300
00:20:39,881 --> 00:20:41,980
and now depends primarily on machines.

301
00:20:41,981 --> 00:20:48,980
But when the going really gets tough, there is another method.

302
00:20:56,980 --> 00:20:57,980
Explosives.

303
00:20:57,981 --> 00:21:02,980
And today, one that gives the most bang for the buck is ammonium nitrate fertilizer.

304
00:21:02,981 --> 00:21:05,980
Ammonium nitrate, it's AMFO.

305
00:21:05,981 --> 00:21:10,980
We use a high density called FragMax, and we mix it with motor oil.

306
00:21:10,981 --> 00:21:15,980
Most people use diesel fuel, but this comes mixed with motor oil.

307
00:21:16,980 --> 00:21:21,980
This blast site today is probably used anywhere from 18,000 to 20,000 pounds.

308
00:21:23,980 --> 00:21:28,980
It was back in 1867 that Swedish chemist Alfred Nobel

309
00:21:28,981 --> 00:21:34,980
revolutionized the earth-moving industry with his new invention, dynamite.

310
00:21:35,980 --> 00:21:39,980
Dynamite, if you may or may not know, is nitroglycerin.

311
00:21:39,981 --> 00:21:45,180
that has been packaged in with sawdust or something

312
00:21:45,181 --> 00:21:47,840
that takes carry the nitroglycerin, make it safer.

313
00:21:48,400 --> 00:21:51,080
Dynamite made it possible to move mountains.

314
00:21:56,080 --> 00:21:58,340
Alfred Nobel's explosive invention

315
00:21:58,341 --> 00:22:00,500
also quickly became an effective weapon.

316
00:22:01,540 --> 00:22:04,280
But this wasn't the only time earth-moving technology

317
00:22:04,281 --> 00:22:05,620
was adopted by the military.

318
00:22:05,621 --> 00:22:13,620
Benjamin Holt had invented the Caterpillar tractor for peacetime use, but its development

319
00:22:13,621 --> 00:22:15,934
was propelled forward by war.

320
00:22:15,935 --> 00:22:20,960
Even before America entered World War I, General John Blackjack

321
00:22:20,961 --> 00:22:26,900
Pershing used Holt's crawler tractors to supply his troops as he pursued legendary bandit

322
00:22:26,901 --> 00:22:30,157
Pancho Villa across the Mexican border.

323
00:22:30,158 --> 00:22:34,500
Pershing never caught Villa, but the tractors proved

324
00:22:34,501 --> 00:22:38,760
their worth as rugged cargo carriers.

325
00:22:38,761 --> 00:22:41,040
August 1914.

326
00:22:41,041 --> 00:22:44,080
As war loomed over Europe, British General E.D.

327
00:22:44,081 --> 00:22:46,980
Swinton proposed building an armed land destroyer

328
00:22:46,981 --> 00:22:50,820
on a caterpillar-type tractor base.

329
00:22:50,821 --> 00:22:54,420
The young first lord of the admiralty, Winston Churchill,

330
00:22:54,421 --> 00:22:56,700
was quick to see the potential of this technology

331
00:22:56,701 --> 00:22:59,560
to create a formidable new weapon.

332
00:22:59,561 --> 00:23:00,960
And the tank was born.

333
00:23:04,500 --> 00:23:08,500
The dictates of combat also accelerated the development of

334
00:23:08,501 --> 00:23:12,500
everything from large cranes to giant steam shovels.

335
00:23:12,501 --> 00:23:16,500
Entrepreneur Alonzo Pauling teamed up with German immigrant

336
00:23:16,501 --> 00:23:19,500
Henry Harnischfeger to found a company that would profit

337
00:23:19,501 --> 00:23:22,500
immensely from selling their powerful earthmovers to America's

338
00:23:22,501 --> 00:23:26,500
armed forces. Their company, P&H, would eventually

339
00:23:26,501 --> 00:23:29,500
become the world's largest manufacturer of power ships.

340
00:23:29,501 --> 00:23:34,360
Their company, P&H, would eventually become the world's largest manufacturer of power

341
00:23:34,361 --> 00:23:38,740
shovels, huge excavators, and hydraulic drills.

342
00:23:38,741 --> 00:23:41,500
War is a pretty good money-making proposition.

343
00:23:41,501 --> 00:23:44,500
You know, certainly Holt and Best got rich off the idea.

344
00:23:44,501 --> 00:23:49,440
But, you know, after the war, the markets got a little thin.

345
00:23:49,441 --> 00:23:55,060
Huge unsold inventories and canceled war contracts led longtime rivals Holt and Best

346
00:23:55,061 --> 00:23:58,260
to bury the hatchet and make peace as well.

347
00:23:58,261 --> 00:24:00,800
In a few years, the two companies merged,

348
00:24:00,801 --> 00:24:04,600
but they kept the winning Caterpillar trademark.

349
00:24:04,601 --> 00:24:07,600
If you're a good operator, you would be called a cat skinner,

350
00:24:07,601 --> 00:24:09,600
which I think kind of denotes the popularity

351
00:24:09,601 --> 00:24:11,240
of Caterpillar equipment.

352
00:24:11,241 --> 00:24:13,540
There were a lot of other manufacturers,

353
00:24:13,541 --> 00:24:16,580
but when you say Caterpillar, that's kind of generic

354
00:24:16,581 --> 00:24:20,020
for any crawler tractor.

355
00:24:20,021 --> 00:24:22,680
In the 1920s, improved mechanization

356
00:24:22,681 --> 00:24:26,260
signaled a new era of American road building.

357
00:24:26,261 --> 00:24:29,140
millions of federal and state dollars poured into new roads

358
00:24:29,141 --> 00:24:31,780
for the tin lizzies being churned out by Henry Ford

359
00:24:31,781 --> 00:24:34,260
and cars being produced by other automakers.

360
00:24:36,200 --> 00:24:38,860
A new generation of improved bulldozers,

361
00:24:39,180 --> 00:24:41,060
scrapers, excavators, and rollers

362
00:24:41,061 --> 00:24:43,520
changed the look of the American countryside.

363
00:24:44,820 --> 00:24:46,960
And as road construction boomed,

364
00:24:46,961 --> 00:24:51,240
these new tools grew in weight, power, and popularity.

365
00:24:51,880 --> 00:24:54,100
This is a Caterpillar 60,

366
00:24:54,101 --> 00:24:58,600
And it's, you know, this built a lot of America.

367
00:24:58,900 --> 00:25:02,760
It's the last of the big gas burners up through the 20s.

368
00:25:02,780 --> 00:25:05,240
This one was built, I believe, 28 to 32.

369
00:25:05,900 --> 00:25:09,380
And it's, you know, it's very typical of earlier Caterpillars.

370
00:25:10,380 --> 00:25:12,860
Big, low RPM gas engine.

371
00:25:13,220 --> 00:25:14,800
Simple, very rugged.

372
00:25:15,860 --> 00:25:18,680
One returning veteran, Bob Letourneau,

373
00:25:19,040 --> 00:25:21,680
came back to California broke and in debt.

374
00:25:21,681 --> 00:25:27,020
But he borrowed a whole tractor and began to design a new kind of earth scraper.

375
00:25:27,980 --> 00:25:31,240
With each refinement, Letourneau perfected his scraper.

376
00:25:32,760 --> 00:25:36,980
Before long, he was making big rigs like this 1929 Highboy,

377
00:25:37,620 --> 00:25:41,240
equipped with enormous steel wheels being pulled by a Caterpillar tractor.

378
00:25:43,140 --> 00:25:48,100
Later, in a major breakthrough, Letourneau realized that big pneumatic rubber tires

379
00:25:48,101 --> 00:25:51,000
led to increased speed and greater hauling ability.

380
00:25:51,680 --> 00:25:55,680
So by the early 1930s, another wave of innovative scrapers

381
00:25:55,780 --> 00:25:57,680
and haulers was going to work.

382
00:25:57,780 --> 00:26:01,180
He didn't start small and then develop into larger machines.

383
00:26:01,280 --> 00:26:04,120
Very often, the first machine of a type he would build

384
00:26:04,220 --> 00:26:07,520
would be the largest ever built.

385
00:26:07,620 --> 00:26:11,140
By the end of the 1930s, America's earthmovers

386
00:26:11,240 --> 00:26:13,440
would pave the way for the first of the nation's

387
00:26:13,540 --> 00:26:19,240
new superhighways, the Pennsylvania Turnpike.

388
00:26:19,241 --> 00:26:22,580
Contractors' fleets moved 26 million cubic yards

389
00:26:22,680 --> 00:26:24,740
of Pennsylvania Earth.

390
00:26:24,840 --> 00:26:29,080
This new divided road was a glimpse of things to come.

391
00:26:29,180 --> 00:26:31,280
Other even more gigantic earthmoving projects

392
00:26:31,380 --> 00:26:35,180
were part of Franklin Roosevelt's New Deal.

393
00:26:35,280 --> 00:26:37,100
In response to the grave unemployment

394
00:26:37,200 --> 00:26:39,720
of the Great Depression, the new president

395
00:26:39,820 --> 00:26:41,600
was determined to use the government

396
00:26:41,700 --> 00:26:45,200
to put the country back to work.

397
00:26:45,300 --> 00:26:48,440
His administration oversaw a series of earthmoving projects

398
00:26:48,441 --> 00:26:51,440
projects that dwarfed everything that came before them.

399
00:26:56,440 --> 00:26:59,380
In a desolate canyon on the border between Nevada and

400
00:26:59,420 --> 00:27:03,480
Arizona, engineers were facing the daunting task of completing

401
00:27:03,520 --> 00:27:05,020
Hoover Dam.

402
00:27:05,060 --> 00:27:08,460
I was a young pup, a young kid, of course, and I got to see

403
00:27:08,500 --> 00:27:10,520
equipment that I couldn't believe.

404
00:27:10,560 --> 00:27:14,440
A lot of it was made for that job and that job only.

405
00:27:14,460 --> 00:27:17,440
And it was one of the wonders of the world.

406
00:27:17,441 --> 00:27:23,260
It would probably equate with the Egyptians building the pyramids and so forth.

407
00:27:25,620 --> 00:27:34,660
You saw these huge, huge trucks that carried not only debris and you might say blasted rock,

408
00:27:34,800 --> 00:27:43,200
but they also carried huge pipes for the downside and the intakes of the power plant.

409
00:27:43,201 --> 00:27:45,060
It was truly amazing.

410
00:27:47,440 --> 00:27:51,140
Older style trucks were completely inadequate for a job

411
00:27:51,240 --> 00:27:52,740
like this.

412
00:27:52,840 --> 00:27:55,640
An entirely new generation of Mack trucks was designed,

413
00:27:55,740 --> 00:27:58,980
with ripped bodies curved inward at the bottom to withstand

414
00:27:59,080 --> 00:28:01,280
the impact of huge rocks.

415
00:28:01,380 --> 00:28:05,000
The company of Mack developed one of their famous trucks.

416
00:28:05,100 --> 00:28:09,400
And these trucks were chain drive, very strongly built.

417
00:28:09,500 --> 00:28:13,700
And they were really the first trucks designed solely for the

418
00:28:13,701 --> 00:28:19,460
They were really the first trucks designed solely for off-highway use, as opposed to

419
00:28:19,461 --> 00:28:24,680
the lighter-duty highway-type trucks.

420
00:28:24,681 --> 00:28:29,140
The same mastery of earth-moving technology went into building the Grand Coulee Dam on

421
00:28:29,141 --> 00:28:34,920
the Columbia, and hundreds of new dams that dotted the Tennessee Valley and other parts

422
00:28:34,921 --> 00:28:38,360
of the country.

423
00:28:38,361 --> 00:28:41,300
No one in the 1930s knew how important the new dams would

424
00:28:41,301 --> 00:28:45,400
become a few years later.

425
00:28:45,401 --> 00:28:49,360
Without electric power, America couldn't have transformed itself

426
00:28:49,361 --> 00:28:52,800
into Roosevelt's arsenal of democracy.

427
00:28:52,801 --> 00:28:55,000
And earthmovers would play an important role

428
00:28:55,001 --> 00:28:58,320
in the epic conflict looming on the horizon.

429
00:29:01,660 --> 00:29:04,160
The largest drag line working in North America

430
00:29:04,161 --> 00:29:06,420
is known as Ursa Major.

431
00:29:06,421 --> 00:29:11,780
It carries a 160 cubic yard bucket on a 360 foot boom

432
00:29:11,781 --> 00:29:15,280
and weighs in at just under 15 million pounds.

433
00:29:15,281 --> 00:29:17,480
Earth Movers will return on Modern Marvels.

434
00:29:22,080 --> 00:29:24,900
With the invention of steam power and then

435
00:29:24,901 --> 00:29:27,360
electrical and diesel powered equipment,

436
00:29:27,361 --> 00:29:30,160
our ability to move Earth and transform the landscape

437
00:29:30,161 --> 00:29:32,580
accelerated enormously.

438
00:29:32,581 --> 00:29:35,960
Earth moving equipment is big business.

439
00:29:35,961 --> 00:29:40,960
And at every industry expo, a new generation of sophisticated equipment is debuted.

440
00:29:49,080 --> 00:29:51,760
Behind me is the Lieber R994B.

441
00:29:51,761 --> 00:29:56,040
It weighs approximately 300 metric tons.

442
00:29:56,041 --> 00:30:00,680
It has an 18 cubic meter bucket on it.

443
00:30:00,681 --> 00:30:06,200
The major advantage of this machine is that it features all the most up-to-date technologies

444
00:30:06,201 --> 00:30:10,520
we have in our large series of Lieber excavators.

445
00:30:11,300 --> 00:30:18,840
In 2005, the big news was mini machines for use by both professionals and do-it-yourselfers.

446
00:30:18,841 --> 00:30:26,100
These machines, powered, are no bigger than a wheelbarrow, but they travel on tracks,

447
00:30:26,101 --> 00:30:31,360
and many of these machines are designed to fit through just a standard doorway,

448
00:30:31,361 --> 00:30:37,700
but their power and their hydraulics are unmatched by a man with a pick and a shovel.

449
00:30:39,020 --> 00:30:41,800
Another recent innovation is driverless equipment

450
00:30:41,801 --> 00:30:45,620
that can be used in dangerous situations like deep excavations

451
00:30:45,621 --> 00:30:47,560
where high walls could collapse.

452
00:30:48,460 --> 00:30:52,580
Comatsu is committed to being on the forefront of technology moving into the next decade.

453
00:30:52,581 --> 00:30:59,580
For example, we've developed dozers such as the D375A-5 that are fully remote operated.

454
00:30:59,920 --> 00:31:06,600
So an operator can stand 100 yards away from his machine and operate that dozer at that location.

455
00:31:08,040 --> 00:31:12,760
The radio control box is very similar to what you would find inside the cab.

456
00:31:13,160 --> 00:31:17,760
There is a work equipment control lever and a forward and reverse lever.

457
00:31:17,761 --> 00:31:21,660
It's very similar to what a child would use with a radio control car.

458
00:31:22,580 --> 00:31:28,760
The whole point of radio-controlled dozers or radio-controlled equipment in general is

459
00:31:28,761 --> 00:31:33,740
to keep the operator safe. Earth-moving equipment is incorporating

460
00:31:33,741 --> 00:31:40,960
many innovations first developed for the defense industry, including GPS.

461
00:31:40,961 --> 00:31:46,134
In the past, earthmovers have played an important role in many military operations.

462
00:31:46,135 --> 00:31:46,835
Even before

463
00:31:46,836 --> 00:31:51,500
America's entry into the Second World War, its earthmoving industry was gearing up for

464
00:31:51,501 --> 00:31:52,673
war production.

465
00:31:52,674 --> 00:31:58,460
Throughout 1940 and 41, heavy equipment was already being shipped overseas

466
00:31:58,461 --> 00:32:01,802
to the nation's British and Russian allies.

467
00:32:01,803 --> 00:32:05,300
American military designers asked Caterpillar

468
00:32:05,301 --> 00:32:08,245
to design a radically new air-cooled tank.

469
00:32:08,246 --> 00:32:11,260
A few months later, a test model was ready.

470
00:32:12,020 --> 00:32:14,600
Soon, M4 tanks were rolling off the assembly line.

471
00:32:14,601 --> 00:32:21,600
This new diesel tank could use heavy oil and low-octane fuel, an advantage on hilly terrain.

472
00:32:29,600 --> 00:32:36,600
After Japan's surprise attack at Pearl Harbor on December 7, 1941, the entire American earthmoving industry went to war.

473
00:32:36,601 --> 00:32:40,040
industry went to war.

474
00:32:40,140 --> 00:32:42,840
Earthmovers embarked on crash programs,

475
00:32:42,940 --> 00:32:46,440
laying new pipelines, building war materiel plants,

476
00:32:46,540 --> 00:32:48,540
and new highways.

477
00:32:48,640 --> 00:32:52,440
With Japanese forces on the move,

478
00:32:52,540 --> 00:32:56,360
Alaska was vulnerable to attack and invasion.

479
00:32:56,460 --> 00:32:58,960
The military saw a vital need for a highway

480
00:32:59,060 --> 00:33:02,160
to help resupply Alaska through Canada.

481
00:33:02,260 --> 00:33:05,000
Work began on the Alcan Highway, one of the greatest

482
00:33:05,001 --> 00:33:09,040
the greatest construction projects since the Panama Canal.

483
00:33:09,041 --> 00:33:10,880
The men who scrambled to build it dubbed it

484
00:33:10,881 --> 00:33:13,820
the Road to Tokyo.

485
00:33:13,821 --> 00:33:17,220
It was built in only two short summer seasons.

486
00:33:17,221 --> 00:33:18,460
It was a major effort.

487
00:33:18,461 --> 00:33:22,440
And they forged through swamps and woods and creeks

488
00:33:22,441 --> 00:33:27,440
and pushed this road through in just a phenomenal pace.

489
00:33:27,441 --> 00:33:30,440
By 1942, the first Mack trucks roared

490
00:33:30,441 --> 00:33:35,440
on the completed Alcan Highway to Fairbanks.

491
00:33:35,540 --> 00:33:38,440
Earthmovers proved indispensable in the Pacific,

492
00:33:38,540 --> 00:33:41,440
where the US was island-hopping its way toward Japan.

493
00:33:46,540 --> 00:33:49,940
The Navy Seabees gained renown for their ability

494
00:33:50,040 --> 00:33:54,940
to build bases and landing strips virtually overnight.

495
00:33:55,040 --> 00:33:58,440
This is an international TD-14.

496
00:33:58,441 --> 00:34:02,440
These tractors were developed primarily for agricultural purposes.

497
00:34:02,441 --> 00:34:08,440
One of the interesting things, or certainly lucky things for us in World War II,

498
00:34:08,441 --> 00:34:11,440
is this heavy construction equipment's readily adaptable.

499
00:34:11,441 --> 00:34:17,440
Actually, there was very little change in equipment as it went onto the battlefield.

500
00:34:17,441 --> 00:34:21,440
The importance of modern earth-moving machines was dramatically apparent in China,

501
00:34:21,441 --> 00:34:26,440
where local laborers had to build emergency landing strips for American fliers,

502
00:34:26,441 --> 00:34:31,200
using the same slow and labor-intensive earth-moving methods as their ancestors.

503
00:34:33,300 --> 00:34:39,480
Admiral William Bull Halsey acknowledged earth-moving technology's contribution to the final victory.

504
00:34:40,700 --> 00:34:51,200
He said the four machines that won the war in the Pacific were the submarine, radar, the airplane, and the tractor bulldozer.

505
00:34:51,201 --> 00:34:53,600
bulldozer.

506
00:34:53,601 --> 00:34:57,800
By war's end, companies like Bucyrus Erie and Marion

507
00:34:57,801 --> 00:35:01,520
were manufacturing earth-moving giants, the likes of which had

508
00:35:01,521 --> 00:35:02,340
never been seen.

509
00:35:08,540 --> 00:35:10,720
The giant dipper of one of these shovels

510
00:35:10,721 --> 00:35:14,660
could hold a Chevrolet truck or nine men.

511
00:35:14,661 --> 00:35:17,020
With the demands of wartime production,

512
00:35:17,021 --> 00:35:20,760
enormous new excavators, drag lines,

513
00:35:20,761 --> 00:35:24,020
and steam dredges were developed that dwarfed everything

514
00:35:24,021 --> 00:35:26,000
that had come before.

515
00:35:26,001 --> 00:35:31,080
And this was only a taste of things to come.

516
00:35:31,081 --> 00:35:35,560
The Caterpillar 797B dump truck's tires are 13 feet tall

517
00:35:35,561 --> 00:35:37,920
and weigh almost 6 tons each.

518
00:35:37,921 --> 00:35:41,460
They need to be replaced after approximately 35,000 miles

519
00:35:41,461 --> 00:35:45,140
at a cost of $25,000 each.

520
00:35:45,141 --> 00:35:47,260
Earthmovers will return on Modern Marvels.

521
00:35:50,760 --> 00:35:55,760
Mining is often Earth moving on its most epic scale.

522
00:35:55,761 --> 00:36:00,760
It's one Earth's natural resources, fossil fuels, and precious metals

523
00:36:00,761 --> 00:36:05,760
we have searched, torn into, and sifted through the surface of our planet.

524
00:36:05,761 --> 00:36:13,760
In one of Earth moving's more unique operations, oil is being mined, not drilled for,

525
00:36:13,761 --> 00:36:17,760
in the oil-saturated sands of central Canada.

526
00:36:20,760 --> 00:36:23,400
The important thing here is the bitumen, which

527
00:36:23,500 --> 00:36:29,240
is an asphalt-like oil substance that is thick as molasses.

528
00:36:29,340 --> 00:36:34,080
But it's trapped in with all the sand and debris and clay

529
00:36:34,180 --> 00:36:37,980
and water, hence the term oil sand.

530
00:36:38,080 --> 00:36:41,640
This is mined by shovels and trucks.

531
00:36:44,420 --> 00:36:46,160
The excavated material is hauled

532
00:36:46,260 --> 00:36:49,320
to a plant where conveyor belts carry it to vats,

533
00:36:49,321 --> 00:36:52,920
where the oil is separated from the soil.

534
00:36:52,921 --> 00:36:56,460
It takes approximately 2 metric tons of oil sand

535
00:36:56,461 --> 00:37:02,560
to produce a single barrel of light-sweet Canadian crude oil.

536
00:37:02,561 --> 00:37:05,400
And there is a lot of it up there.

537
00:37:05,401 --> 00:37:09,480
The combined output of the four major oil sand fields

538
00:37:09,481 --> 00:37:14,040
found in Alberta have the potential of producing oil

539
00:37:14,041 --> 00:37:18,140
that is five times greater than the conventional reserves

540
00:37:18,141 --> 00:37:24,280
reserves currently found in Saudi Arabia.

541
00:37:24,281 --> 00:37:26,880
Most of the truly gigantic earth moving machines

542
00:37:26,881 --> 00:37:29,280
are manufactured for mining purposes,

543
00:37:29,281 --> 00:37:32,660
a trend that began more than 80 years ago.

544
00:37:32,661 --> 00:37:36,900
The very earliest stripping shovel used in coal mining

545
00:37:36,901 --> 00:37:40,260
was developed by the Marion Steam Shovel Company.

546
00:37:40,261 --> 00:37:42,400
They came out almost every two or three years

547
00:37:42,401 --> 00:37:44,200
with a bigger machine.

548
00:37:44,201 --> 00:37:48,140
And by 1923, they had made the world's largest

549
00:37:48,141 --> 00:37:50,980
ever to move on land.

550
00:37:51,080 --> 00:37:54,240
That was quite phenomenal because it was built in the days

551
00:37:54,340 --> 00:37:56,780
before the era of welding.

552
00:37:56,880 --> 00:37:59,580
This huge machine was built using rivets.

553
00:37:59,680 --> 00:38:03,480
This was quite a major breakthrough.

554
00:38:03,580 --> 00:38:05,700
In the years following World War II,

555
00:38:05,800 --> 00:38:09,800
enormous strip mining machines were manufactured.

556
00:38:09,900 --> 00:38:13,440
The biggest increases in size occurred during the 1950s and

557
00:38:13,540 --> 00:38:16,240
60s.

558
00:38:16,241 --> 00:38:19,640
More than 40 years after it was first manufactured,

559
00:38:19,740 --> 00:38:22,340
a stripping shovel called the Silver Spade

560
00:38:22,440 --> 00:38:25,340
remains one of the largest ever built.

561
00:38:25,440 --> 00:38:28,280
It's the last still operational from the era

562
00:38:28,380 --> 00:38:29,780
of supersized giants.

563
00:38:35,800 --> 00:38:39,100
It was erected at a mine in Ohio for the sole purpose

564
00:38:39,200 --> 00:38:41,800
of removing earth and rock to uncover coal.

565
00:38:46,240 --> 00:38:52,040
Designed and built by Bucir-Siri, the same company that built Big Muskie,

566
00:38:52,840 --> 00:38:57,640
the Silver Spade has been one of the favorite big stripping shovels.

567
00:38:58,160 --> 00:39:00,360
Even though it was not the largest,

568
00:39:01,340 --> 00:39:04,460
any shovel that stands over 20 stories tall,

569
00:39:05,260 --> 00:39:07,940
weighs 7,200 tons,

570
00:39:07,941 --> 00:39:12,820
and carries a bucket that's 105 cubic yards in capacity

571
00:39:12,821 --> 00:39:15,640
is large in anybody's part.

572
00:39:16,240 --> 00:39:21,240
The length of the Silver Spade's boom is 200 feet.

573
00:39:21,241 --> 00:39:26,240
Its series of driving motors total 9,000 horsepower

574
00:39:26,241 --> 00:39:31,240
and are powered by 7,200 volts of electricity.

575
00:39:35,240 --> 00:39:38,240
One day the Silver Spade will be shut down.

576
00:39:38,241 --> 00:39:39,240
It's inevitable.

577
00:39:39,241 --> 00:39:41,240
Its age is against it.

578
00:39:41,241 --> 00:39:45,240
And it will be a sad day when that day does come.

579
00:39:46,240 --> 00:39:51,280
Not only is it the last super stripping shovel in operation today in the world,

580
00:39:51,700 --> 00:39:54,920
it is the last stripping shovel, period.

581
00:39:55,560 --> 00:39:59,180
And when it shuts down, it will be an end of an era.

582
00:40:00,760 --> 00:40:05,000
Economic and environmental concerns that have developed over the last 30 years

583
00:40:05,001 --> 00:40:07,300
doomed the Silver Spades' fellow giants.

584
00:40:08,480 --> 00:40:13,580
Coal strip mining was devastating vast areas of Ohio, Arkansas, and other states.

585
00:40:14,460 --> 00:40:16,700
Not bound by any reclamation laws,

586
00:40:17,500 --> 00:40:19,160
mining companies had no obligation

587
00:40:19,161 --> 00:40:21,900
to restore the scarred landscapes they left behind.

588
00:40:23,220 --> 00:40:26,460
When I was a child in a small rural community in Arkansas,

589
00:40:26,680 --> 00:40:28,080
just a few miles out of town,

590
00:40:28,760 --> 00:40:30,500
we had a strip mining operation.

591
00:40:30,640 --> 00:40:33,720
It was a subsidiary of a large national coal mining company.

592
00:40:34,140 --> 00:40:35,360
Back during the Depression,

593
00:40:35,960 --> 00:40:39,720
you saw that as a good job that paid $1 to $4 an hour,

594
00:40:40,300 --> 00:40:42,960
which was a big salary, big wage back then.

595
00:40:43,580 --> 00:40:45,980
and they saw it in one dimensional.

596
00:40:45,981 --> 00:40:47,260
And that was pure economics.

597
00:40:47,261 --> 00:40:50,460
Nobody was thinking about the long-term effects of the damage

598
00:40:50,461 --> 00:40:52,220
this earth moving equipment was doing.

599
00:40:55,260 --> 00:40:59,980
The problems with strip mining had been accelerating in the 50s and 60s

600
00:40:59,981 --> 00:41:04,860
in Appalachia, and yet it wasn't until in 1972

601
00:41:04,861 --> 00:41:10,220
there was a failure of a dam that coal miners had built up on a hillside

602
00:41:10,221 --> 00:41:12,300
in a place called Buffalo Creek in West Virginia.

603
00:41:12,301 --> 00:41:18,700
and the material behind that dam all flooded down into the valley and drowned 125 people.

604
00:41:20,780 --> 00:41:25,920
Disasters like this compelled legislation that had a dramatic impact on the coal companies.

605
00:41:26,940 --> 00:41:31,080
Some major strip mining machines like Big Muskie were closed down.

606
00:41:32,860 --> 00:41:36,780
Mining companies also developed cost-effective ways to remove the topsoil,

607
00:41:37,380 --> 00:41:41,240
the layer called the overburden, mine the coal seam,

608
00:41:41,241 --> 00:41:44,960
and then comply with regulations to restore the land.

609
00:41:47,280 --> 00:41:51,720
Soon, most of the industry moved out of Appalachia to western states like Wyoming,

610
00:41:52,480 --> 00:41:54,620
where the coal was more economical to mine.

611
00:41:56,880 --> 00:42:01,280
Out west, different types of mining operations search for other buried treasures.

612
00:42:02,480 --> 00:42:07,620
Near Elko, Nevada, some of the biggest equipment on earth works the enormous Betsy Pit Mine,

613
00:42:07,621 --> 00:42:11,920
an immense and very rich $10 billion gold deposit.

614
00:42:12,900 --> 00:42:17,360
Huge trucks haul ore out of the pit at the rate of 240 tons a load.

615
00:42:18,600 --> 00:42:23,280
The mammoth project's efficiency is enhanced by a variety of computer-coordinated equipment.

616
00:42:24,840 --> 00:42:29,460
Using massive pumps, earth-moving engineers keep water from refilling the pit,

617
00:42:30,060 --> 00:42:32,760
improving the efficiency and speed of ore extraction.

618
00:42:32,761 --> 00:42:42,924
In the last 15 years, the mining industry, in fact, has cut its manpower demands in the U.S.

619
00:42:42,925 --> 00:42:44,360
by about 80%.

620
00:42:44,361 --> 00:42:51,780
So we now only employ about 50,000 people to do the mining that used to require a quarter of a million people.

621
00:42:52,160 --> 00:42:55,420
And yet we're mining metals at a greater rate than ever before.

622
00:42:55,421 --> 00:43:04,140
Huge mining operations encourage the 100-year-old P&H company to recently add a new behemoth

623
00:43:04,141 --> 00:43:05,200
to its product line.

624
00:43:06,580 --> 00:43:12,400
This impressive drag line called the 90-20 has a crane longer than a football field

625
00:43:12,401 --> 00:43:15,420
and swings a 100-cubic-yard bucket.

626
00:43:17,620 --> 00:43:24,920
It moves on state-of-the-art walking shoes and sports its own built-in kitchen.

627
00:43:25,420 --> 00:43:37,040
Because of this ever-increasing ability to move the earth, industry leaders and policymakers

628
00:43:37,041 --> 00:43:39,920
face new challenges and new responsibilities.

629
00:43:39,921 --> 00:43:47,720
The new earth-moving equipment gives us the opportunity to create bigger and bigger

630
00:43:47,721 --> 00:43:53,000
environmental messes, but it also gives us the opportunity to fix them.

631
00:43:55,960 --> 00:44:00,200
In the future, new technologies and the ongoing public policy debate

632
00:44:01,160 --> 00:44:03,880
will lead to enormous changes in the earth moving industry.

633
00:44:07,480 --> 00:44:13,640
In the meantime, the world's impressive arsenal of earth movers and the talented professionals

634
00:44:13,641 --> 00:44:17,640
that man them will continue to transform our planet.

635
00:44:22,640 --> 00:44:24,480
As it came over the park, it just fell apart.

636
00:44:24,481 --> 00:44:27,280
There was a fireman who actually was videotaping.

637
00:44:27,281 --> 00:44:29,580
There was anonymously left in our mailbox.

638
00:44:29,581 --> 00:44:34,700
If this tape is real, we will have the first recorded evidence

639
00:44:34,701 --> 00:44:37,300
of a UFO crash in American history.

